<SubSection title="Types of Charge" id="Types of Charge">
<p>The electron, proton, and neutron are fundamental particles that make up all atoms. Electrons and protons have an elementary charge equal in magnitude but opposite in sign. <b>Charge</b> is denoted by <i>q</i> and is expressed in Coulombs. One elementary charge equals <InlineEquation equation="1.602 x 10^{-19}C" />. </p>
 <p class="text-center"><em>Fundamental Particles</em></p>
  <DisplayTable id="dim-table">
    <thead>
      <tr>
        <th>Fundamental Particle</th><th>Mass (g)</th><th>Elementary Charge</th><th>Charge (C)</th>
      </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <th>Electron</th><td><InlineEquation equation="9.11 x 10^{-28}" /></td><td>-1</td><td><InlineEquation equation="-1.602 x 10^{-19}" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Proton</th><td><InlineEquation equation="1.67 x 10^{-24}" /></td><td>+1</td><td><InlineEquation equation="+1.602 x 10^{-19}" /></td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <th>Neutron</th><td><InlineEquation equation="1.67 x 10^{-24}" /></td><td>0</td><td>0</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </DisplayTable>

</SubSection>

